                                    <h2><span class="shaft"><img id="1772-0" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_c.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Cardioid" title="Cardioid" ></span>Placid Audio Copperphone Mini</h2>
                    
                    <h2 class="sub">                    Cardioid Dynamic Microphone</h2>
                    <p>The Placid Audio Copperphone Mini is a physically smaller version of the company&#8217;s Copperphone model. Both are &#8220;effect microphones,&#8221; intended to color and modify the sound of the source. Effects mics are often used in conjunction with less-colored microphones, with the intention of blending in the effect mic to best suit the production.</p><p>The Mini has a different moving-coil cartridge than the Copperphone. It described as &#8220;higher performance&#8221; by Placid Audio. The Mini also lacks the Copperphone&#8217;s resonant chamber. However, the sound characteristics are similar in that both are limited-bandwidth microphones, suitable for putting a nostalgic color on recordings and performances. </p><p>Designer Mark Pirro originally conceived the Copperphone Mini as a hand-held &#8220;bullet&#8221; microphone for harmonica players. The size of the mic &#8212; 58mm by 44mm &#8212; is conducive to hand holding, and the limited-bandwidth sound, similar to that of the Copperphone, is naturally suited to a limited-bandwidth instrument like the harmonica.</p><p>However, unlike some dedicated harmonica mics, the Copperphone Mini terminates in a Switchcraft XLR connector rather than a 1/4-inch guitar plug. The XLR output allows for for greater flexibility in the studio and onstage where XLR connections are expected, but the mic can still be plugged into a guitar amplifier via the use of a XLRF to 1/4&#8221; impedance converter, such as the Whirlwind Little IMP (http://whirlwindusa.com/catalog/black-boxes-effects-and-dis/transformers-isolation-devices/little-imp). Placid Audio offers examples of this sound on their website.</p><p>The mic is sold already installed inside an aircraft aluminum ring mount, which is built to the same rugged standard as the mic itself. The ring mount acts as a functional shock mount for the mic, and also gives the mic a visually vintage character with its 1930&#8217;s styling, reminiscent of a Shure 5b carbon mic. (In part due to its distinctive styling, the band Rush made use of the mic as a crowd mic for their 2010 Time Machine tour.) If the ring is not desired, conversion hardware is included to remove it from the ring for use as a handheld mic or for closer placement to the source.</p><p>Like all Placid Audio microphones, the Copperphone Mini has a lifetime warranty.<p>Permalink: <a href="//recordinghacks.com/microphones/PlacidAudio/CopperphoneMini">Placid Audio CopperphoneMini</a></p><p>The mic was released in 2015.</p>
